function helpPostgresql() {
    topic <<'EOF'
The database: PostgreSQL                                                [ROOT]

  The stone needs an empty database and a role which owns it; it creates its
  tables itself at the first start. It neither needs nor should have the
  right to create databases or roles. Whoever runs the database server sets
  this up once; after that, the stone only needs the URL and the password.

  A database server which already exists elsewhere needs only step 2 there,
  and step 4 here.

  1. AS ROOT - install the server on this host:

    Debian, Ubuntu:
      # apt install postgresql
    Fedora, RHEL, Rocky, Alma:
      # dnf install postgresql-server
      # postgresql-setup --initdb
      # systemctl enable --now postgresql

  2. AS ROOT - the role and the database, with a password of your own:

      # sudo -u postgres psql
      postgres=# CREATE ROLE eds_user WITH LOGIN PASSWORD 'a long password';
      postgres=# CREATE DATABASE eds WITH OWNER = eds_user;

  3. AS ROOT - let the container reach a server on this host. In the
     container, 'localhost' is the container itself; the host is
     'host.containers.internal', and a connection to it doesn't reach a
     server which only listens on the loopback address. So the server must
     also listen on an address of the host, and accept the role from it.

     In postgresql.conf (its place: SHOW config_file; in psql):
       listen_addresses = 'localhost,<address of this host>'

     In pg_hba.conf (SHOW hba_file;), one line for the stone:
       host  eds  eds_user  <address of this host>/32  scram-sha-256

      # systemctl restart postgresql

     The port 5432 must not be reachable from other hosts unless that is
     wanted: keep it closed in the firewall.

  4. AS YOU - tell the stone, in @ENV@:

       EDS_DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://host.containers.internal:5432/eds
       EDS_DB_USERNAME=eds_user
       EDS_DB_PASSWORD=a long password

      $ @SCRIPT@ restart
      $ @SCRIPT@ status             Database: PostgreSQL

  A stone which can't reach its database doesn't fall back to memory: it
  stays not ready and tries again, by default every ten seconds, and says so
  in its log ('@SCRIPT@ logs'). All that is stored is in the database, so the
  backup of the stone is the backup of this database, with the MasterKey.
EOF
}

function helpMasterKey() {
    topic <<'EOF'
The MasterKey

  Everything a stone stores is protected by its MasterKey, which is never
  stored itself: the stone reads it at the start, and without it stays
  locked. Whoever loses it loses all that is stored. There are three ways to
  give it, and the choice is who can start the stone:

  1. The MasterKey file on this host. The stone starts by itself, also with
     the host. Whoever can read your files can read it.

      $ @SCRIPT@ masterkey generate
       STONE_MASTERKEY=file          in @ENV@ (the default)

  2. A source elsewhere, which the stone reads at each start. Taking the
     source away keeps the stone from starting again: a kill-switch.

       STONE_MASTERKEY=none
       EDS_MASTERKEY_URL=https://keys.example.com/stone-north

  3. By hand, after each start. The stone starts locked, and the System
     Administrator unlocks it; nothing of the MasterKey stays on the host
     when the file is kept elsewhere.

       STONE_MASTERKEY=none
      $ @SCRIPT@ start
      $ @SCRIPT@ masterkey load     Asks for the name and the passphrase
                                    of the System Administrator

  The file is @HOME@/masterkey, readable only by you.
  Keep a copy of it in a safe place, apart from the backup of the database:
  one is of no use without the other. 'masterkey generate' never replaces a
  file unless --force is given, as what the old MasterKey protected can then
  no longer be opened.
EOF
}

function helpTls() {
    topic <<'EOF'
HTTPS by the stone itself

  The stone can serve HTTPS without anything in front of it, which needs no
  root as long as the port is 1024 or above. For the ports 80 and 443, and
  for certificates which renew themselves, a reverse proxy is the usual way:
  see '@SCRIPT@ help proxy'.

  1. Put the certificate, with its chain, and the key here, readable by you:

       @HOME@/certs/tls.crt
       @HOME@/certs/tls.key

  2. In @ENV@:

       STONE_BIND=0.0.0.0
       STONE_TLS_PORT=8443
       EDS_TLS_CERT=/app/certs/tls.crt
       EDS_TLS_KEY=/app/certs/tls.key
       EDS_INSECURE_REQUESTS=redirect

     The two paths are those in the container, where the directory is
     mounted read-only. EDS_INSECURE_REQUESTS tells what happens to plain
     HTTP: enabled (answered), redirect (sent to HTTPS) or disabled.

  3.  $ @SCRIPT@ restart

  The stone is then at https://<host>:8443/. A renewed certificate is used
  from the next restart.
EOF
}

function helpProxy() {
    topic <<'EOF'
HTTPS by a reverse proxy                                                [ROOT]

  The usual way to the world outside: the stone listens only on this host
  (STONE_BIND=127.0.0.1, the default), and a reverse proxy in front of it
  holds the ports 80 and 443 and the certificate. Installing and setting it
  up changes the system and needs root; the stone itself stays as it is.

  What the proxy must do, whichever it is:
    - pass everything on to http://127.0.0.1:@PORT@, with the Host header
    - pass on WebSocket upgrades, for /api/v1/socket (the chat and calls)
    - allow bodies up to the largest file of the stone (1000 MiB by default)
      and pass them on while they arrive, without storing them first
    - allow a request to last as long as a large file takes
    - never pass on the port 9000 (health), which the host doesn't publish

  AS ROOT - with Caddy, which gets and renews the certificate itself:

      # apt install caddy             or: dnf install caddy

    /etc/caddy/Caddyfile:
      stone.example.com {
          request_body {
              max_size 1100MB
          }
          reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:@PORT@ {
              flush_interval -1
          }
      }

      # systemctl reload caddy

  AS ROOT - with nginx, and a certificate from e.g. certbot:

      # apt install nginx             or: dnf install nginx

    /etc/nginx/conf.d/stone.conf:
      map $http_upgrade $connection_upgrade {
          default upgrade;
          ''      close;
      }
      server {
          listen 443 ssl;
          server_name stone.example.com;
          ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/stone.example.com/fullchain.pem;
          ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/stone.example.com/privkey.pem;

          client_max_body_size 1100m;
          proxy_request_buffering off;
          proxy_buffering off;
          proxy_read_timeout 3600s;
          proxy_send_timeout 3600s;

          location / {
              proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:@PORT@;
              proxy_http_version 1.1;
              proxy_set_header Host $host;
              proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
              proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;
          }
      }
      server {
          listen 80;
          server_name stone.example.com;
          return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
      }

      # nginx -t && systemctl reload nginx

    On a system with SELinux (Fedora, RHEL), nginx may only connect to the
    stone after:
      # setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect 1

  AS ROOT - open the firewall for 80 and 443, and for nothing else:

      # ufw allow 80,443/tcp                                     (Ubuntu)
      # firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http --add-service=https
      # firewall-cmd --reload                                    (Fedora, RHEL)

  AS YOU - nothing changes; the stone keeps EDS_INSECURE_REQUESTS=enabled, as
  the proxy speaks plain HTTP to it on this host only.

      $ @SCRIPT@ status

  The stone answers below https://stone.example.com/api/v1.
EOF
}

function helpBoot() {
    topic <<'EOF'
Start with the host                                               [ROOT, once]

  '@SCRIPT@ enable' makes a service of your own user which starts the stone,
  with systemd: @UNIT@.service. That needs no root. But the services
  of a user only run while that user is logged in, unless the user may
  'linger', which root allows once for the user, however many stones:

  AS ROOT - this changes the system:

      # loginctl enable-linger @USER@

  AS YOU:

      $ @SCRIPT@ enable             Start with the host
      $ @SCRIPT@ disable            No longer; the stone keeps running
      $ systemctl --user status @UNIT@

  What happens when the host starts again:

    1. systemd starts the services of every user who may linger, without a
       login. Each enabled instance has its own, so all of them start; one
       which fails doesn't keep the others from starting.
    2. The service waits for the network where Podman can tell, starts the
       container and is done. If the container can't be started yet, it
       tries again every ten seconds.
    3. The stone itself waits for its database: one which isn't up yet is
       tried again until it answers, so the order of the two doesn't
       matter. PostgreSQL on this host starts with the host by itself, once
       root has enabled it (systemctl enable postgresql).
    4. The stone reads its MasterKey, from the file or another source (see
       '@SCRIPT@ help masterkey'), and is unlocked. Without a source it
       starts locked, and waits for '@SCRIPT@ masterkey load': with
       STONE_MASTERKEY=none, a host which starts again needs you.

  So for a stone to be back by itself after a start of the host, it needs:
  linger for the user, '@SCRIPT@ enable', a database which starts by
  itself, and a MasterKey source. '@SCRIPT@ check' tells which is missing.
  Try it without starting the host again:

      $ @SCRIPT@ stop
      $ systemctl --user start @UNIT@
      $ @SCRIPT@ status

  The service calls this script where it is now. After moving the script,
  run '@SCRIPT@ enable' again, for each instance.
EOF
}

function helpInstances() {
    topic <<'EOF'
Several stones on one host

  A host may run several stones, e.g. one for each group of people. Each is
  an instance with a name, given before the command:

      $ @PROGRAM@ -i north init
      $ @PROGRAM@ -i south init
      $ @PROGRAM@ list

  Without -i it is the default instance, which is all a host with one
  stone needs. STONE_INSTANCE=north names it for a whole shell.

  An instance has, of its own:

                     the default instance       the instance 'north'
    Settings         ~/.config/stone/           ~/.config/stone/north/
    MasterKey file   ~/.config/stone/masterkey  ~/.config/stone/north/masterkey
    Logs             ~/.local/state/stone/logs  ~/.local/state/stone/north/logs
    Container        stone                      stone-north
    Service          stone.service              stone-north.service

  What each instance must be given, as nothing can be shared:

    - A port of its own on the host, STONE_PORT in its settings (and
      STONE_TLS_PORT, if it serves HTTPS itself). 'init' proposes the next
      one which is free, and 'check' tells if two instances have the same.
    - A database of its own: another name in EDS_DB_URL, and best a role
      of its own too. See '@PROGRAM@ help postgresql'; the steps 1 and 3
      are done once for the host, step 2 for each stone:
        postgres=# CREATE ROLE north_user WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '...';
        postgres=# CREATE DATABASE north WITH OWNER = north_user;
      and one more line in pg_hba.conf for it.
    - A MasterKey of its own: '@PROGRAM@ -i north masterkey generate'.
    - A name of its own in the reverse proxy: one site for each stone,
      passing on to its port. See '@PROGRAM@ help proxy'.

  What they share: Podman, the images (several instances run from the same
  image), and the right to linger, which root gives once for the user.

  Each instance is started with the host by itself:

      $ @PROGRAM@ -i north enable
      $ @PROGRAM@ -i south enable

  Every command is for one instance: 'start', 'stop', 'status', 'logs',
  'check', 'masterkey' and 'cleanup'. Only 'list' and 'load' are for all of
  them, and 'cleanup' keeps an image another instance still runs from.
EOF
}

function helpCleanup() {
    topic <<'EOF'
Removing what is no longer used

  Loading a newer image leaves the one it replaced on the host, without a
  name. Nothing uses it, and it takes its space until it is removed:

      $ @SCRIPT@ cleanup            Removes EDS images which a newer one
                                    replaced. The stone keeps running

  To take the stone off the host:

      $ @SCRIPT@ cleanup --images   Stops and removes the container, and
                                    removes all EDS images
      $ @SCRIPT@ cleanup --all      The same, and the logs in
                                    @LOGS@,
                                    and the start with the host

  Each asks before it removes anything, unless --yes is given. Only images
  of EDS are touched, never other images or containers of yours.

  None of them removes the settings or the MasterKey file in
  @HOME@: without the MasterKey, what the
  database holds can never be read again, so that file is only ever removed
  by hand. The database is not touched either; it is removed by whoever
  runs it:

      # sudo -u postgres dropdb eds                               [ROOT]
EOF
}

# ==============================================================================
# Set up
# ==============================================================================
function doInit() {
    [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || { usageError "Unknown option: ${1}"; return 1; }

    (umask 077 && mkdir -p "${stoneHome}" "${certsDir}" "${logsDir}") || return 1
    if [[ -e "${envFile}" ]]; then
        echo "The settings file ${envFile} already exists, it is kept."
        return 0
    fi

    # The next port no other instance has in its settings, from 8080 on
    local free=8080 other taken=" "
    while IFS= read -r other; do
        taken+="$(settingIn "${stoneBase}${other:+/${other}}/stone.env" STONE_PORT 8080) "
    done < <(instances)
    while [[ "${taken}" == *" ${free} "* ]]; do
        free=$((free + 1))
    done

    (umask 077 && cat > "${envFile}" <<EOF
# Settings of $(instanceName) on this host, see '${script} help settings'.
# One setting per line, NAME=value, without quotes; comments on their own lines.

# The address and the port of the host the stone listens on. 127.0.0.1 is
# only this host, which is right behind a reverse proxy; 0.0.0.0 is every
# address of the host.
STONE_BIND=127.0.0.1
STONE_PORT=${free}

# Start with the MasterKey file (${script} masterkey generate): file or none
STONE_MASTERKEY=file

# The database, see '${script} help postgresql'. Without EDS_DB_URL the stone
# keeps everything in memory, and loses it when it stops.
#EDS_DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://host.containers.internal:5432/eds
#EDS_DB_USERNAME=eds_user
#EDS_DB_PASSWORD=

# HTTPS by the stone itself, see '${script} help tls'
#STONE_TLS_PORT=8443
#EDS_TLS_CERT=/app/certs/tls.crt
#EDS_TLS_KEY=/app/certs/tls.key
#EDS_INSECURE_REQUESTS=redirect
EOF
    ) || return 1
    echo "Created ${envFile}, on port ${free}."
    echo "Next: the database in that file ('${script} help postgresql'), then '${script} check'."
}

function doLoad() {
    [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || { usageError "Usage: ${script} load <archive>"; return 1; }
    needPodman || return 1
    if [[ ! -f "${1}" ]]; then
        echo "${1} not found."
        return 1
    fi
    podman load -q -i "${1}" || return 1
    # A running stone keeps the image it was started from
    if isRunning; then
        local used present
        used=$(podman container inspect -f '{{.Image}}' "$(containerName)" 2>/dev/null)
        present=$(podman image inspect -f '{{.Id}}' "$(image)" 2>/dev/null)
        if [[ "${used}" != "${present}" ]]; then
            echo "The running stone still uses its old image, '${script} restart' replaces it."
        fi
    fi
}

# Tells what is in place and what is missing, and who can do what is missing
function doCheck() {
    echo "Stone check${instance:+ of '${instance}'}"
    echo
    echo "Podman"
    if ! command -v podman > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        missing "Podman is not installed"
        todoRoot "Install it, see '${script} help podman'"
    else
        ok "$(podman --version)"
        local user="${USER:-$(id -un)}"
        if grep -q "^${user}:" /etc/subuid 2>/dev/null && grep -q "^${user}:" /etc/subgid 2>/dev/null; then
            ok "Subordinate ids for ${user}"
        else
            missing "No subordinate user or group ids for ${user}, rootless containers can't start"
            todoRoot "usermod --add-subuids 100000-165535 --add-subgids 100000-165535 ${user}"
        fi
        if podman info > /dev/null 2>&1; then
            ok "Podman runs as ${user}, without root"
        else
            missing "'podman info' fails for ${user}"
            todoUser "See its message; after a change of the ids: podman system migrate"
        fi
    fi

    echo
    echo "Image"
    local wanted
    wanted=$(image)
    if command -v podman > /dev/null 2>&1 && imageExists "${wanted}"; then
        ok "${wanted}"
    else
        missing "${wanted} is not on this host"
        todoUser "${script} load <archive>, see '${script} help images'"
    fi

    echo
    echo "Settings"
    if [[ -f "${envFile}" ]]; then
        ok "${envFile}"
        if [[ -n "$(find "${envFile}" -perm /077 2>/dev/null)" ]]; then
            missing "The settings file can be read by others"
            todoUser "chmod 600 ${envFile}"
        fi
    else
        missing "No settings file, the defaults are used"
        todoUser "${script} init"
    fi

    local address number
    address=$(bindAddress)
    number=$(port)
    if [[ "${number}" -lt 1024 ]]; then
        local first
        first=$(cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_unprivileged_port_start 2>/dev/null)
        if [[ -n "${first}" && "${number}" -lt "${first}" ]]; then
            missing "Port ${number} is below ${first}, which only root may use"
            todoUser "Use a port from 1024 on (STONE_PORT) behind a reverse proxy, see '${script} help proxy'"
            todoRoot "Or: sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start=${number}"
        else
            ok "Listens on ${address}:${number}"
        fi
    elif [[ "${address}" == 127.0.0.1 ]]; then
        ok "Listens on ${address}:${number}, only reachable from this host"
        todoRoot "For the world outside: a reverse proxy, see '${script} help proxy'"
    else
        ok "Listens on ${address}:${number}"
    fi

    # Two instances can't listen on the same port of the host
    local other otherFile
    while IFS= read -r other; do
        [[ "${other}" == "${instance}" ]] && continue
        otherFile="${stoneBase}${other:+/${other}}/stone.env"
        if [[ "$(settingIn "${otherFile}" STONE_PORT 8080)" == "${number}" ]]; then
            missing "The instance '${other:-default}' has the same port ${number}; only one of the two can run"
            todoUser "Another STONE_PORT in ${envFile}"
        fi
    done < <(instances)

    echo
    echo "Database"
    local url
    url=$(setting EDS_DB_URL)
    if [[ -z "${url}" ]]; then
        missing "No database (EDS_DB_URL): everything is kept in memory and lost when the stone stops"
        todoRoot "A PostgreSQL database, see '${script} help postgresql'"
        todoUser "Then EDS_DB_URL, EDS_DB_USERNAME and EDS_DB_PASSWORD in the settings file"
    else
        ok "${url}"
        [[ -n "$(setting EDS_DB_PASSWORD)" ]] || missing "No password (EDS_DB_PASSWORD)"
        checkDatabaseListens "${url}"
    fi

    echo
    echo "MasterKey"
    if [[ -n "$(setting EDS_MASTERKEY_URL)" ]]; then
        ok "Read at the start from $(setting EDS_MASTERKEY_URL)"
    elif [[ "$(setting STONE_MASTERKEY file)" == file ]]; then
        if [[ -s "${masterKeyFile}" ]]; then
            ok "Read at the start from ${masterKeyFile}"
            if [[ -n "$(find "${masterKeyFile}" -perm /077 2>/dev/null)" ]]; then
                missing "The MasterKey file can be read by others"
                todoUser "chmod 600 ${masterKeyFile}"
            fi
        else
            missing "No MasterKey file, the stone starts locked"
            todoUser "${script} masterkey generate, see '${script} help masterkey'"
        fi
    else
        ok "None at the start: the stone starts locked, until '${script} masterkey load'"
    fi

    echo
    echo "Start with the host"
    if ! command -v systemctl > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        missing "No systemd on this host, start the stone your own way"
    else
        if isEnabled; then
            ok "The service ${unitName}.service is enabled"
        else
            missing "The stone is not started with the host"
            todoUser "${script} enable"
        fi
        if [[ "$(loginctl show-user "${USER:-$(id -un)}" -p Linger --value 2>/dev/null)" == yes ]]; then
            ok "Your services run without you being logged in (linger)"
        else
            missing "Your services only run while you are logged in"
            todoRoot "loginctl enable-linger ${USER:-$(id -un)}, see '${script} help boot'"
        fi
    fi
    echo
    echo "[USER] is done by you; [ROOT] changes the system and needs root."
}

# A PostgreSQL on this host must listen on more than the loopback address,
# or the container can't reach it. Only a hint: it is checked where it can be.
function checkDatabaseListens() {
    local url="${1}" rest host number
    rest="${url#jdbc:postgresql://}"
    rest="${rest%%/*}"
    host="${rest%%:*}"
    number=5432
    [[ "${rest}" == *:* ]] && number="${rest##*:}"
    case "${host}" in
        localhost | 127.0.0.1)
            missing "In the container, ${host} is the container itself, not this host"
            todoUser "Use host.containers.internal in EDS_DB_URL for a database on this host"
            ;;
        host.containers.internal)
            command -v ss > /dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
            local listeners
            listeners=$(ss -ltnH 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $4}' | grep -E ":${number}\$")
            if [[ -z "${listeners}" ]]; then
                missing "Nothing listens on port ${number} of this host"
                todoRoot "Install and start PostgreSQL, see '${script} help postgresql'"
            elif ! echo "${listeners}" | grep -qvE '^(127\.|\[::1\])'; then
                missing "Port ${number} only listens on the loopback address, which the container can't reach"
                todoRoot "listen_addresses and pg_hba.conf, see '${script} help postgresql' (step 3)"
            else
                ok "Port ${number} of this host listens for the container"
            fi
            ;;
    esac
}

# ==============================================================================
# Run
# ==============================================================================
function doStart() {
    # --no-wait is for the service: it starts the container and is done, as
    # the stone itself waits for its database.
    local wait=true
    for option in "$@"; do
        case "${option}" in
            --no-wait) wait=false ;;
            *) usageError "Unknown option: ${option}"; return 1 ;;
        esac
    done
    needPodman || return 1

    local name wanted
    name=$(containerName)
    wanted=$(image)
    if isRunning; then
        echo "$(instanceName Capital) is already running."
        return 0
    fi
    if ! imageExists "${wanted}"; then
        echo "The image ${wanted} is not on this host."
        echo "Load it with '${script} load <archive>', see '${script} help images'."
        return 1
    fi

    local withMasterKey=false
    if [[ -z "$(setting EDS_MASTERKEY_URL)" && "$(setting STONE_MASTERKEY file)" == file && -s "${masterKeyFile}" ]]; then
        withMasterKey=true
    fi

    # A stopped container is replaced, so the present image and settings are
    # always used. Nothing is lost: all that is stored is in the database.
    podman rm "${name}" > /dev/null 2>&1
    (umask 077 && mkdir -p "${logsDir}") || return 1

    # The "eds" user of the image (UID/GID 1001) is mapped to you, so the
    # logs are yours. The proxy settings of the host are not passed on, as
    # the stone only connects to its database and the MasterKey source. The
    # :Z and :z options relabel the mounts for SELinux, and are ignored
    # without it. The health port 9000 is not published.
    local address
    address=$(bindAddress)
    local args=(run -d --name "${name}" --http-proxy=false
        --userns=keep-id:uid=1001,gid=1001
        -p "${address}:$(port):8080"
        -v "${logsDir}:/app/logs:Z"
        -e "TZ=$(setting TZ "$(cat /etc/timezone 2>/dev/null || echo UTC)")"
        -e QUARKUS_LOG_FILE_ENABLED=true
        -e QUARKUS_LOG_FILE_PATH=/app/logs/eds.log
        -e QUARKUS_LOG_FILE_ROTATION_MAX_FILE_SIZE=10M
        -e QUARKUS_LOG_FILE_ROTATION_MAX_BACKUP_INDEX=5)

    # The settings file first, so the environment takes precedence. Passed
    # by name, so the values, like the password, are not in the process list.
    [[ -f "${envFile}" ]] && args+=(--env-file "${envFile}")
    local variable
    for variable in EDS_DB_URL EDS_DB_USERNAME EDS_DB_PASSWORD EDS_DB_RETRY_INTERVAL \
                    EDS_MASTERKEY_URL EDS_TLS_CERT EDS_TLS_KEY EDS_INSECURE_REQUESTS; do
        [[ -n "${!variable+set}" ]] && args+=(-e "${variable}")
    done
    if [[ -n "$(setting EDS_TLS_CERT)" ]]; then
        args+=(-p "${address}:$(tlsPort):8443" -v "${certsDir}:/app/certs:ro,z")
    fi
    # The MasterKey file is mounted read-only and used as the MasterKey
    # source; the secret itself is never part of the command line.
    if [[ "${withMasterKey}" == true ]]; then
        args+=(-v "${masterKeyFile}:${masterKeyMount}:ro,z" -e "EDS_MASTERKEY_URL=file://${masterKeyMount}")
    fi

    echo "Starting $(instanceName)..."
    podman "${args[@]}" "${wanted}" > /dev/null || return 1
    [[ "${wait}" == true ]] || return 0
    waitForStone
}

function servedAt() {
    local base
    base="http://$(localAddress):$(port)"
    echo "the API at ${base}/api/v1"
}

function waitForStone() {
    echo -n "Waiting for the stone "
    local retries=0
    while [[ ${retries} -lt 60 ]]; do
        if ! isRunning; then
            echo
            echo "Error: the stone stopped. Its last words:"
            podman logs --tail 15 "$(containerName)" 2>&1 | sed 's/^/  | /'
            return 1
        fi
        if isReady; then
            echo
            echo "EDS $(edsVersion) is ready: $(servedAt)"
            return 0
        fi
        echo -n "."
        sleep 1
        retries=$((retries + 1))
    done

    echo
    echo "The stone runs, but is not ready after 60 seconds. It keeps trying when"
    echo "it can't reach its database; see '${script} logs' and '${script} check'."
    return 1
}

function doStop() {
    [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || { usageError "Unknown option: ${1}"; return 1; }
    needPodman || return 1
    if isRunning; then
        echo "Stopping the stone..."
        podman stop "$(containerName)" > /dev/null
    else
        echo "The stone is not running."
    fi
}

function doStatus() {
    needPodman || return 1
    echo "Stone status${instance:+ of '${instance}'}"
    echo
    local wanted
    wanted=$(image)
    if imageExists "${wanted}"; then
        ok "Image: ${wanted}, created $(podman image inspect -f '{{.Created.Format "2006-01-02 15:04"}}' "${wanted}" 2>/dev/null)"
    else
        missing "Image ${wanted} is not on this host, see '${script} help images'"
    fi

    if isRunning; then
        local used
        used=$(podman container inspect -f '{{.ImageName}}' "$(containerName)" 2>/dev/null)
        ok "Container: running, from ${used#localhost/}"
        if isReady; then
            ok "Health: UP"
        else
            echo "  [FAIL] Health: not ready, see '${script} logs'"
        fi
        local answer
        answer=$(inContainer --header "Content-Type: application/json" --post-data '{}' "http://localhost:8080/api/v1/version")
        if [[ -n "${answer}" ]]; then
            ok "EDS $(echo "${answer}" | grep -o '"version":"[^"]*"' | cut -d'"' -f4): $(servedAt)"
            local database
            database=$(echo "${answer}" | grep -o '"database":"[^"]*"' | cut -d'"' -f4)
            if echo "${answer}" | grep -q '"persistent":true'; then
                ok "Database: ${database}"
            else
                missing "Database: ${database}, in memory: everything is lost when the stone stops"
            fi
        else
            echo "  [FAIL] The API doesn't answer"
        fi
    elif containerExists; then
        missing "Container: stopped, start it with '${script} start'"
    else
        missing "Container: not created, start it with '${script} start'"
    fi

    if isEnabled; then
        ok "Starts with the host (${unitName}.service)"
    else
        missing "Not started with the host, see '${script} help boot'"
    fi
    echo
}

function doLogs() {
    needPodman || return 1
    if ! containerExists; then
        echo "The stone was not started yet, start it with '${script} start'."
        return 1
    fi
    echo "Following the log of the stone (Ctrl+C to exit)..."
    podman logs -f --tail 100 "$(containerName)"
}

# ==============================================================================
# Start with the host: a systemd service of the user, which needs no root.
# That it also runs without a login (linger) is allowed by root, once.
# ==============================================================================
function doEnable() {
    command -v systemctl > /dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "This host has no systemd."; return 1; }
    local self
    self=$(readlink -f "${0}")
    mkdir -p "${unitDir}" || return 1
    # The network of the system is not known to the services of a user;
    # Podman has a service which waits for it, where it is new enough. Where
    # it isn't, or the container can't be started yet, the start is tried
    # again. The stone itself waits for its database.
    cat > "${unitFile}" <<EOF
# Written by '${script} enable', removed by '${script} disable'
[Unit]
Description=EDS stone${instance:+ (${instance})}
Wants=podman-user-wait-network-online.service
After=podman-user-wait-network-online.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
Environment=STONE_HOME=${stoneBase}
Environment=STONE_STATE=${stateBase}
ExecStart=${self}${instance:+ -i ${instance}} start --no-wait
ExecStop=${self}${instance:+ -i ${instance}} stop
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10
TimeoutStartSec=180

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
EOF
    systemctl --user daemon-reload || return 1
    systemctl --user enable "${unitName}.service" > /dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
    echo "$(instanceName Capital) is started with the host, by ${unitFile}."
    if ! isRunning; then
        echo "It is not running now: '${script} start', or 'systemctl --user start ${unitName}'."
    fi
    if [[ "$(loginctl show-user "${USER:-$(id -un)}" -p Linger --value 2>/dev/null)" != yes ]]; then
        echo
        echo "[ROOT] Your services only run while you are logged in. For the stone to"
        echo "       start with the host, root allows it once (this changes the system):"
        echo "         # loginctl enable-linger ${USER:-$(id -un)}"
    fi
}

function doDisable() {
    command -v systemctl > /dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "This host has no systemd."; return 1; }
    systemctl --user disable "${unitName}.service" > /dev/null 2>&1
    rm -f "${unitFile}"
    systemctl --user daemon-reload
    echo "$(instanceName Capital) is no longer started with the host."
}

# ==============================================================================
# The instances of this user, and what each is doing
# ==============================================================================
function doList() {
    local names=() name
    mapfile -t names < <(instances)
    if [[ ${#names[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then
        echo "No stone is set up yet: '${program} init', or '${program} -i <name> init'."
        return 0
    fi
    printf '  %-12s %-22s %-10s %s\n' "INSTANCE" "LISTENS ON" "STATE" "WITH THE HOST"
    local file container state boot
    for name in "${names[@]}"; do
        file="${stoneBase}${name:+/${name}}/stone.env"
        container=$(settingIn "${file}" STONE_NAME "stone${name:+-${name}}")
        state="stopped"
        if command -v podman > /dev/null 2>&1 \
                && [[ "$(podman container inspect -f '{{.State.Running}}' "${container}" 2>/dev/null)" == "true" ]]; then
            state="running"
        fi
        boot="no"
        if command -v systemctl > /dev/null 2>&1 \
                && systemctl --user is-enabled "stone${name:+-${name}}.service" > /dev/null 2>&1; then
            boot="yes"
        fi
        printf '  %-12s %-22s %-10s %s\n' "${name:-(default)}" \
            "$(settingIn "${file}" STONE_BIND 127.0.0.1):$(settingIn "${file}" STONE_PORT 8080)" "${state}" "${boot}"
    done
}

# ==============================================================================
# MasterKey
# ==============================================================================

# Creates a random MasterKey secret: 48 random bytes, Base64 encoded (64
# characters, 384 bits), well above the entropy EDS requires. EDS uses the
# bytes of the file as they are, so no newline is written.
function generateMasterKey() {
    local force=false
    for option in "$@"; do
        case "${option}" in
            --force) force=true ;;
            *) usageError "Unknown option: ${option}"; return 1 ;;
        esac
    done

    if [[ -e "${masterKeyFile}" && "${force}" != true ]]; then
        echo "The MasterKey file ${masterKeyFile} already exists, it is kept."
        echo "Replacing it makes the data protected by it unreadable; use --force if that is intended."
        return 1
    fi

    local secret
    secret=$(head -c 48 /dev/urandom | base64 -w0) || return 1
    (umask 077 && mkdir -p "${stoneHome}" && printf '%s' "${secret}" > "${masterKeyFile}") || return 1
    chmod 600 "${masterKeyFile}"
    echo "Created the MasterKey file ${masterKeyFile} (readable only by you)."
    echo "Keep a copy of it in a safe place: without it, what is stored can't be read."
    echo "The stone reads it at the next '${script} start' (STONE_MASTERKEY=file)."
}

# Sends the MasterKey file to a running stone with the MasterKey request. On
# a new stone, it becomes the first MasterKey, and the admin account is
# created with the given passphrase; later, it unlocks a stone which started
# without a source. The secret and the passphrase are sent via stdin, so they
# don't show in the process list.
function loadMasterKey() {
    command -v curl > /dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "This needs curl."; return 1; }
    if [[ ! -s "${masterKeyFile}" ]]; then
        echo "The MasterKey file ${masterKeyFile} doesn't exist."
        echo "Create one with '${script} masterkey generate'."
        return 1
    fi
    if ! isRunning; then
        echo "The stone is not running, start it first."
        return 1
    fi

    # A stone sets no name for its System Administrator: on a new stone the
    # name given here becomes it, later it is the name it has.
    local account="$(setting STONE_ADMIN_ACCOUNT "${EDS_ADMIN_ACCOUNT:-}")"
    if [[ -z "${account}" ]]; then
        read -r -p "Account name of the System Administrator (a new stone gives it this name) [admin]: " account
        account="${account:-admin}"
    fi
    if [[ "${account}" == *'"'* || "${account}" == *'\'* ]]; then
        echo "The account name can't hold a quote or a backslash here."
        return 1
    fi
    local passphrase="${EDS_ADMIN_PASSPHRASE:-}"
    if [[ -z "${passphrase}" ]]; then
        read -r -s -p "Passphrase of ${account}: " passphrase
        echo
    fi
    if [[ -z "${passphrase}" ]]; then
        echo "No passphrase given."
        return 1
    fi

    local response returnCode message
    response=$(printf '{"accountName":"%s","credential":"%s","secret":"%s"}' "${account}" \
            "$(printf '%s' "${passphrase}" | base64 -w0)" "$(base64 -w0 < "${masterKeyFile}")" \
        | curl -s -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data-binary @- "$(apiUrl)/masterKey")
    returnCode=$(echo "${response}" | grep -o '"returnCode":[0-9]*' | cut -d: -f2)
    message=$(echo "${response}" | grep -o '"returnMessage":"[^"]*"' | cut -d'"' -f4)

    if [[ "${returnCode}" == "200" ]]; then
        echo "${message:-MasterKey loaded.}"
        return 0
    fi
    echo "The MasterKey was not loaded: ${message:-no answer from the stone} (${returnCode:-?})"
    return 1
}

# ==============================================================================
# Clean up
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only what is of EDS is touched: its images carry a label. The settings and
# the MasterKey file are never removed.
# ==============================================================================

# The EDS images which no longer have a name, as a newer one replaced them
function replacedImages() {
    podman images -q --filter "dangling=true" --filter "label=${edsLabel}" 2>/dev/null | sort -u
}

# All EDS images, with or without a name
function allImages() {
    {
        podman images -q --filter "label=${edsLabel}" 2>/dev/null
        local custom
        custom=$(setting STONE_IMAGE)
        [[ -n "${custom}" ]] && podman images -q "${custom}" 2>/dev/null
    } | sort -u
}

function listImages() {
    local id line tags
    for id in "$@"; do
        line=$(podman image inspect -f '{{.RepoTags}}|{{.Created.Format "2006-01-02 15:04"}}' "${id}" 2>/dev/null) || continue
        tags="${line%%|*}"
        tags="${tags#[}"
        tags="${tags%]}"
        echo "    ${id:0:12}  ${line##*|}  ${tags:-(replaced by a newer one)}"
    done
}

function doCleanup() {
    local mode=unused yes=false
    for option in "$@"; do
        case "${option}" in
            --images) mode=images ;;
            --all) mode=all ;;
            --yes) yes=true ;;
            *) usageError "Unknown option: ${option}"; return 1 ;;
        esac
    done
    needPodman || return 1

    local ids=()
    if [[ "${mode}" == unused ]]; then
        mapfile -t ids < <(replacedImages)
        if [[ ${#ids[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then
            echo "No EDS images which are no longer used."
            return 0
        fi
        echo "EDS images which a newer one replaced:"
        listImages "${ids[@]}"
        confirmed "${yes}" "Remove them?" || { echo "Nothing removed."; return 0; }
        # An image which a container still uses is kept by Podman
        podman rmi "${ids[@]}" > /dev/null 2>&1
        mapfile -t ids < <(replacedImages)
        if [[ ${#ids[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then
            echo "Kept, as the stone still runs from it ('${script} restart' uses the new one):"
            listImages "${ids[@]}"
        else
            echo "Removed."
        fi
        return 0
    fi

    mapfile -t ids < <(allImages)
    echo "This takes $(instanceName) off this host:"
    containerExists && echo "  the container '$(containerName)'$(isRunning && echo ", which is running")"
    if [[ ${#ids[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then
        echo "  the EDS images, unless another stone has a container from them:"
        listImages "${ids[@]}"
    fi
    if [[ "${mode}" == all ]]; then
        echo "  the logs in ${logsDir}"
        [[ -e "${unitFile}" ]] && echo "  the start with the host (${unitFile})"
    fi
    echo "Kept: the settings and the MasterKey file in ${stoneHome}, and the database."
    confirmed "${yes}" "Go on?" || { echo "Nothing removed."; return 0; }

    if [[ "${mode}" == all && -e "${unitFile}" ]]; then
        doDisable > /dev/null
    fi
    podman rm -f "$(containerName)" > /dev/null 2>&1
    # Without force: an image the container of another instance runs from
    # is kept by Podman.
    local id
    for id in "${ids[@]}"; do
        podman rmi "${id}" > /dev/null 2>&1
    done
    if [[ "${mode}" == all ]]; then
        rm -rf "${logsDir}"
    fi
    echo "Removed."
    mapfile -t ids < <(allImages)
    if [[ ${#ids[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then
        echo "Kept, as another stone on this host has a container from it:"
        listImages "${ids[@]}"
    fi
}

# ==============================================================================
# Main
# ==============================================================================
if [[ "$(id -u)" -eq 0 ]]; then
    echo "Don't run '${script}' as root: the stone runs as a normal user, with rootless"
    echo "Podman. Only the steps marked [ROOT] in '${script} help setup' need root."
    exit 1
fi

command="${1:-}"
[[ $# -gt 0 ]] && shift

function noOptions() {
    [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || usageError "Unknown option: ${1}"
}

case "${command}" in
    "" | -h | --help)
        showHelp
        ;;
    help)
        helpTopic "$@"
        ;;
    init)
        doInit "$@"
        ;;
    check)
        noOptions "$@" && doCheck
        ;;
    list)
        noOptions "$@" && doList
        ;;
    load)
        doLoad "$@"
        ;;
    start)
        doStart "$@"
        ;;
    stop)
        doStop "$@"
        ;;
    restart)
        doStop && doStart "$@"
        ;;
    status)
        noOptions "$@" && doStatus
        ;;
    logs)
        noOptions "$@" && doLogs
        ;;
    enable)
        noOptions "$@" && doEnable
        ;;
    disable)
        noOptions "$@" && doDisable
        ;;
    masterkey)
        action="${1:-}"
        [[ $# -gt 0 ]] && shift
        case "${action}" in
            generate) generateMasterKey "$@" ;;
            load) noOptions "$@" && loadMasterKey ;;
            *) usageError "Unknown command: masterkey ${action}" ;;
        esac
        ;;
    cleanup)
        doCleanup "$@"
        ;;
    *)
        usageError "Unknown command: ${command}"
        ;;
esac
